1
00:00:55,597 --> 00:00:58,809
There are some fish
that cannot be caught.

2
00:01:00,852 --> 00:01:03,981
It's not that they're faster
or stronger than other fish .

3
00:01:04,147 --> 00:01:07,317
They're just touched
by something extra .

4
00:01:08,568 --> 00:01:11,238
One such fish was The Beast.

5
00:01:12,906 --> 00:01:16,994
And by the time I was born
he was aIready a Iegend .

6
00:01:17,160 --> 00:01:21,748
He'd passed up more $1 00 lures
than any fish in AIabama .

7
00:01:23,375 --> 00:01:25,919
Some said that fish was
the ghost of a thief. . .

8
00:01:26,086 --> 00:01:29,298
. . .who'd drowned in that river
60 years before .

9
00:01:30,799 --> 00:01:35,512
Others claimed he was a dinosaur
left over from the "Cruaceous" Period .

10
00:01:35,679 --> 00:01:39,516
I didn't put any stock into
such speculation or superstition .

11
00:01:39,683 --> 00:01:42,352
All I knew was I'd been trying
to catch that fish . . .

12
00:01:42,519 --> 00:01:45,022
. . . since I was a boy
no bigger than you .

13
00:01:45,605 --> 00:01:48,025
And on the day you were born . . . .

14
00:01:48,191 --> 00:01:51,194
Well , that was the day
I finally caught him .

15
00:01:53,071 --> 00:01:54,865
Now, I'd tried everything on it:

16
00:01:55,032 --> 00:01:58,869
Worms , lures , peanut butter,
peanut butter and cheese .

17
00:01:59,036 --> 00:02:01,663
But on that day I had a revelation :

18
00:02:01,830 --> 00:02:06,668
If that fish was Henry Walls' ghost,
then the usual bait wasn't gonna work.

19
00:02:06,835 --> 00:02:11,131
I was gonna have to use something
he truly desired .

20
00:02:12,049 --> 00:02:14,718
-Your finger?
-Gold .

21
00:02:15,552 --> 00:02:18,764
Now, I tied my ring onto
the strongest line they made . . .

22
00:02:18,930 --> 00:02:23,185
. . . strong enough to hold up a bridge ,
they said , if only for a few minutes .

23
00:02:24,227 --> 00:02:27,064
And then I cast upriver.

24
00:02:29,816 --> 00:02:34,446
The Beast jumped up and grabbed it
before it even hit the water.

25
00:02:34,613 --> 00:02:39,076
And just as fast,
he snapped clean through that line .

26
00:02:39,242 --> 00:02:40,911
Well , you can see my predicament.

27
00:02:41,078 --> 00:02:45,082
My wedding ring ,
the symbol of fidelity to my wife . . .

28
00:02:45,332 --> 00:02:48,001
- . . . soon to be mother of my child . . .
-Make him stop .

29
00:02:48,168 --> 00:02:51,088
. . .was now lost in the gut
of an uncatchable fish .

30
00:02:51,421 --> 00:02:52,923
What did you do?

31
00:02:53,090 --> 00:02:56,385
I followed that fish upriver
and downriver.

32
00:02:58,095 --> 00:03:00,514
This fish , The Beast. . .

33
00:03:00,680 --> 00:03:06,645
. . .the whole time we were calling it
a him , when in fact it was a her.

34
00:03:06,812 --> 00:03:09,106
It was fat with eggs
it was gonna lay any day .

35
00:03:09,272 --> 00:03:11,942
Now, I was in a situation .

36
00:03:12,109 --> 00:03:15,779
I could gut that fish
and get my wedding ring back. . .

37
00:03:16,405 --> 00:03:21,201
. . . but in doing so I'd be killing the
smartest catfish in the Ashton River.

38
00:03:21,368 --> 00:03:23,787
Did I want to deprive
my son the chance . . .

39
00:03:23,954 --> 00:03:26,998
. . .to catch a fish like this
of his own?

40
00:03:27,165 --> 00:03:29,793
This ladyfish and I . . . .

41
00:03:29,960 --> 00:03:34,464
-Well , we had the same destiny .
-"We were part of the same equation . "

42
00:03:35,590 --> 00:03:37,676
Now, you may well ask. . .

43
00:03:37,843 --> 00:03:41,596
Oh , darling , darling ,
it's still your night.

44
00:03:41,763 --> 00:03:47,644
. . .why did it strike so quick on gold
when nothing else would attract it?

45
00:03:47,811 --> 00:03:50,939
That was the lesson
I learned that day . . .

46
00:03:51,106 --> 00:03:54,151
. . .the day my son was born .

47
00:03:54,317 --> 00:04:00,365
Sometimes the only way to catch
an uncatchable woman . . .

48
00:04:00,532 --> 00:04:04,077
. . . is to offer her a wedding ring .

49
00:04:12,502 --> 00:04:15,172
What, a father's not allowed
to talk about his son?

50
00:04:15,338 --> 00:04:17,174
I'm a footnote in that story , Dad . . .

51
00:04:17,340 --> 00:04:23,346
. . .the conte xt for your great adventure ,
which never happened , incidentally .

52
00:04:23,513 --> 00:04:26,683
You were selling novelty products
in Wichita when I was born .

53
00:04:26,850 --> 00:04:29,436
Come on , Will .
Everyone loves that story !

54
00:04:29,603 --> 00:04:33,982
They don't. I don't love that story . Not
anymore . Not after a thousand times !

55
00:04:34,149 --> 00:04:38,069
I know every punch line , Dad .
I can tell them as well as you can !

56
00:04:38,236 --> 00:04:41,531
For one night,
one night in your entire life . . .

57
00:04:41,698 --> 00:04:44,868
. . .the universe did not revolve
around Edward Bloom .

58
00:04:45,035 --> 00:04:49,289
-How can you not understand that?
-I'm sorry to embarrass you .

59
00:04:49,456 --> 00:04:53,376
You're embarrassing yourself, Dad .
You just don't see it.

60
00:04:55,504 --> 00:04:59,549
After that night, l didn't speak
to my father again for three years.

61
00:05:05,055 --> 00:05:09,059
William Bloom , U nited Press
I nternational . If I could just--

62
00:05:12,812 --> 00:05:15,398
We communicated indirectly , l guess.

63
00:05:15,565 --> 00:05:19,569
ln her letters and Christmas cards,
my mother wrote for both of them .

64
00:05:19,903 --> 00:05:24,824
And when l'd call , she'd say Dad was
out driving or swimming in the pool .

65
00:05:24,991 --> 00:05:28,036
True to form , we never
talked about not talking .

66
00:05:30,372 --> 00:05:34,751
The truth is, l didn't see anything
of myself in my father.

67
00:05:34,918 --> 00:05:38,255
And l don't think he saw anything
of himself in me.

68
00:05:41,758 --> 00:05:45,387
We were like strangers
who knew each other very well .

69
00:05:50,517 --> 00:05:52,853
ln telling the story
of my father's life . . .

70
00:05:53,019 --> 00:05:57,524
. . . it's impossible to separate fact
from fiction , the man from the myth .

71
00:06:00,318 --> 00:06:04,155
The best l can do
is to tell it the way he told me .

72
00:06:05,907 --> 00:06:09,369
lt doesn't always make sense ,
and most of it never happened .

73
00:06:34,436 --> 00:06:37,105
Give me back my ring !

74
00:06:49,451 --> 00:06:50,994
Thank you !

75
00:06:51,328 --> 00:06:54,539
But that's what kind of story this is.

76
00:07:03,590 --> 00:07:06,843
His birth would set the pace
for his unlikely life .

77
00:07:07,010 --> 00:07:10,055
No longer than most men's,
but larger.

78
00:07:10,221 --> 00:07:12,599
And as strange as his stories got. . .

79
00:07:12,766 --> 00:07:16,269
. . .the endings were always
the most surprising of all .

80
00:07:43,004 --> 00:07:45,757
Yes . Yes , he's here .

81
00:07:46,883 --> 00:07:48,677
It's your mother.

82
00:07:55,642 --> 00:07:57,519
And what does Dr. Bennett say?

83
00:07:59,479 --> 00:08:01,731
No , sure , I'll talk to him .

84
00:08:02,732 --> 00:08:04,734
Yeah , I'll wait.

85
00:08:04,901 --> 00:08:06,403
It's bad?

86
00:08:06,569 --> 00:08:10,699
Yeah , it's more than they thought.
They're gonna stop chemo .

87
00:08:11,908 --> 00:08:14,577
-You need to go .
-Probably tonight.

88
00:08:15,495 --> 00:08:18,999
-I'm going with you .
-No , no , no . You shouldn't.

89
00:08:19,165 --> 00:08:20,959
I'm going with you .

90
00:08:50,280 --> 00:08:52,115
Now, which one's it gonna be?

91
00:08:52,282 --> 00:08:55,118
"Monkey in a Barn , "
or "Dog in the Road"?

92
00:08:55,535 --> 00:08:57,454
The one about the witch .

93
00:08:57,787 --> 00:09:00,749
Your momma says I can't
tell you that one anymore .

94
00:09:00,915 --> 00:09:04,502
-You get nightmares .
-But I'm not scared .

95
00:09:06,296 --> 00:09:08,798
Well , neither was I , at first.

96
00:09:09,299 --> 00:09:12,218
Now, this took place
in the swamp outside of Ashton .

97
00:09:12,385 --> 00:09:17,057
Children weren't allowed in the swamp
on account of the snakes, spiders. . .

98
00:09:17,223 --> 00:09:21,853
. . . and quicksand that would swallow
you up before you could even scream .

99
00:09:22,020 --> 00:09:24,272
There were five of us
out there that night:

100
00:09:24,898 --> 00:09:28,276
Me , Ruthie , Wilbur Freely . . .

101
00:09:28,443 --> 00:09:32,113
. . . and the Price Brothers,
Don and Zacky .

102
00:09:32,280 --> 00:09:36,117
And not one of us knew
what was in store.

103
00:09:36,659 --> 00:09:40,872
lt's common knowledge that most
towns of a certain size have a witch . . .

104
00:09:41,039 --> 00:09:43,249
. . . if only to eat
misbehaving children . . .

105
00:09:43,416 --> 00:09:46,628
. . . and the occasional puppy
who wanders into her yard .

106
00:09:46,795 --> 00:09:49,964
Witches use those bones
to cast spells and curses. . .

107
00:09:50,131 --> 00:09:52,509
. . . and make the land infertile.

108
00:09:52,675 --> 00:09:56,846
-Is it true she got a glass eye?
-I heard she got it from gypsies .

109
00:09:57,013 --> 00:09:59,182
-What's a gypsy?
-Your momma's a gypsy .

110
00:09:59,349 --> 00:10:00,683
Your momma's a bitch .

111
00:10:00,850 --> 00:10:03,061
You shouldn't swear.
There's ladies present.

112
00:10:03,228 --> 00:10:04,479
-Shit.
-Damn .

113
00:10:04,646 --> 00:10:05,688
Screw.

114
00:10:05,855 --> 00:10:07,982
Turn off your flashlights.
She'll see you !

115
00:10:08,149 --> 00:10:10,777
Yet of all the witches in Alabama . . .

116
00:10:10,944 --> 00:10:13,863
. . .there was one who was said
to be the most feared .

117
00:10:14,030 --> 00:10:18,118
For she had one glass eye which
was said to contain mystical powers.

118
00:10:18,284 --> 00:10:22,163
I heard if you look right at it,
you'll see how you're gonna die .

119
00:10:22,330 --> 00:10:26,042
That's bull-S-H-I-T , that is.
She's not even a real witch .

120
00:10:26,209 --> 00:10:28,253
If you're so sure , go get that eye .

121
00:10:28,419 --> 00:10:33,049
I heard she keeps it in a bo x on her
night table . Or are you too scared?

122
00:10:33,216 --> 00:10:35,385
I'll go in right now and get that eye .

123
00:10:35,552 --> 00:10:37,637
-Then do it.
-Fine , I will .

124
00:10:37,804 --> 00:10:40,765
-Fine , you do it.
-Fine , I'm doing it.

125
00:10:40,932 --> 00:10:43,226
Edward , don't!

126
00:10:43,393 --> 00:10:47,313
She'll make soap out of you .
That's what she does.

127
00:10:47,480 --> 00:10:50,149
She makes soap out of people .

128
00:11:39,949 --> 00:11:42,285
Ma'am , my name is Edward Bloom . . .

129
00:11:42,452 --> 00:11:45,121
. . . and there's some folks
like to see your eye .

130
00:11:58,301 --> 00:12:00,261
-You get The Eye?
-I brought it.

131
00:12:00,428 --> 00:12:01,554
Let's see it.

132
00:12:07,185 --> 00:12:09,103
Whoa, help !

133
00:12:18,988 --> 00:12:23,242
I saw how I was gonna die .
I was old and I fell .

134
00:12:23,993 --> 00:12:26,412
I wasn't old at all .

135
00:12:33,753 --> 00:12:37,507
I was thinking about death and all .
About seeing how you're gonna die .

136
00:12:37,674 --> 00:12:41,177
I mean , on one hand ,
if dying was all you thought about. . .

137
00:12:41,469 --> 00:12:43,179
. . . it could kind of screw you up .

138
00:12:43,346 --> 00:12:47,100
But it could kind of help you ,
couldn't it?

139
00:12:47,266 --> 00:12:51,354
Because , everything else ,
you'd know you could survive .

140
00:12:54,190 --> 00:12:57,277
I guess I'm saying I'd like to know.

141
00:13:08,913 --> 00:13:10,748
That's how I go .

142
00:13:33,312 --> 00:13:34,480
H i , Mom .

143
00:13:39,861 --> 00:13:45,616
-Is that Dr. Bennett's car?
-Yes . He's up with your father now.

144
00:13:45,992 --> 00:13:49,495
-And how is he?
-Well-- No , I got it.

145
00:13:49,662 --> 00:13:53,750
He's impossible . He won't eat.
Because he doesn't eat, he's weaker.

146
00:13:53,916 --> 00:13:56,002
Because he's weak,
he doesn't want to eat.

147
00:13:56,169 --> 00:14:01,049
-How much time does he have left?
-You don't talk about that.

148
00:14:01,382 --> 00:14:02,925
Not yet.

149
00:14:04,594 --> 00:14:06,095
Will .

150
00:14:07,180 --> 00:14:10,183
Dr. Bennett. Oh , it's good to see you .

151
00:14:10,350 --> 00:14:12,935
My wife , Josephine .

152
00:14:13,728 --> 00:14:15,104
A pleasure .

153
00:14:15,813 --> 00:14:19,484
-You're seven months .
-To the day !

154
00:14:21,486 --> 00:14:22,779
It's a boy .

155
00:14:25,490 --> 00:14:31,120
Try to get him to drink one of these .
He won't, but go ahead and try .

156
00:15:16,999 --> 00:15:18,292
Dad?

157
00:15:29,679 --> 00:15:31,889
You want some water?

158
00:15:48,573 --> 00:15:52,535
-You are in for a surprise .
-Am I?

159
00:15:52,827 --> 00:15:55,663
Having a kid changes everything .

160
00:15:55,830 --> 00:15:59,584
There's the diapers and the burping
and the midnight feeding .

161
00:15:59,750 --> 00:16:02,753
-Did you do any of that?
-No .

162
00:16:03,379 --> 00:16:05,381
But I hear it's terrible .

163
00:16:05,882 --> 00:16:11,012
Then you spend years trying
to corrupt and mislead this child . . .

164
00:16:11,179 --> 00:16:15,975
. . .fill its head with nonsense ,
and still it turns out perfectly fine .

165
00:16:16,476 --> 00:16:21,105
-You think I'm up for it?
-You learned from the best.

166
00:16:24,984 --> 00:16:27,904
Drink half the can .
I'll tell Mom you drank it all .

167
00:16:28,070 --> 00:16:29,614
Everyone wins .

168
00:16:30,156 --> 00:16:34,076
People needn't worry so much .
It's not my time yet.

169
00:16:34,243 --> 00:16:36,204
This is not how I go .

170
00:16:36,370 --> 00:16:40,583
-Really?
-Truly . I saw it in The Eye .

171
00:16:41,167 --> 00:16:44,545
-The old lady by the swamp?
-She was a witch .

172
00:16:44,712 --> 00:16:47,381
No , she was old and probably senile .

173
00:16:47,548 --> 00:16:52,720
I saw my death in that eye ,
and this isn't how it happens .

174
00:16:52,887 --> 00:16:54,931
So how does it happen?

175
00:16:55,097 --> 00:16:59,685
Surprise ending .
Wouldn't want to ruin it for you .

176
00:17:00,853 --> 00:17:03,731
Your mother thought
we wouldn't talk again .

177
00:17:03,898 --> 00:17:06,692
Look at us. We're talking fine .

178
00:17:06,859 --> 00:17:08,736
We're storytellers , both of us .

179
00:17:08,903 --> 00:17:14,408
I speak mine out,
you write yours down . Same thing .

180
00:17:15,826 --> 00:17:17,495
Dad . . . .

181
00:17:20,289 --> 00:17:22,917
I hope to talk about some things
while I'm here .

182
00:17:23,084 --> 00:17:25,294
You mean while I'm here .

183
00:17:25,753 --> 00:17:29,465
I just want to know
the true versions of things:

184
00:17:29,632 --> 00:17:32,468
Events. Stories.

185
00:17:32,760 --> 00:17:33,970
You .

186
00:17:45,690 --> 00:17:48,317
Your mother hasn't been
keeping up the pool .

187
00:17:48,484 --> 00:17:52,154
-If you wanted to , you could fi x it.
-Yeah , I will .

188
00:17:52,321 --> 00:17:55,574
-You know where the chemicals are?
-I did it when you were gone .

189
00:17:55,741 --> 00:18:01,747
I was never much for being
at home , Will . Too confining .

190
00:18:02,290 --> 00:18:05,001
And this here , being stuck in bed . . . .

191
00:18:05,167 --> 00:18:08,671
Dying is the worst thing
that's ever happened to me .

192
00:18:09,630 --> 00:18:15,303
-I thought you said you weren't dying .
-I said this isn't how I go .

193
00:18:15,469 --> 00:18:19,348
The last part is much more unusual .

194
00:18:19,515 --> 00:18:21,851
Trust me on that.

195
00:18:54,759 --> 00:18:57,720
Dr. Bennett said I have
to stay at home for a week.

196
00:18:57,887 --> 00:19:01,599
Oh , that's nothing . Once ,
I had to stay in bed for three years .

197
00:19:01,766 --> 00:19:05,019
-Did you have the chickenpo x?
-I wish .

198
00:19:24,538 --> 00:19:27,249
Truth is, no one quite knew
what was wrong .

199
00:19:27,583 --> 00:19:30,086
Most times a person
grows up gradually . . .

200
00:19:30,252 --> 00:19:32,588
. . .while l found myself in a hurry .

201
00:19:35,841 --> 00:19:40,012
My muscles and my bones couldn't
keep up with my body's ambition .

202
00:19:40,179 --> 00:19:43,641
So l spent the better part
of three years confined to my bed . . .

203
00:19:43,808 --> 00:19:47,770
. . .with the encyclopaedia being
my only means of ex ploration .

204
00:19:47,937 --> 00:19:49,939
l had made it all the way to the G's. . .

205
00:19:50,106 --> 00:19:52,983
. . . hoping to find an answer
to my "gigantificationism" . . .

206
00:19:53,150 --> 00:19:56,987
. . .when l uncovered an article
about the common goldfish .

207
00:19:57,196 --> 00:20:01,117
"Kept in a small bowl ,
the goldfish will remain small .

208
00:20:01,283 --> 00:20:02,618
With more space . . .

209
00:20:02,785 --> 00:20:06,622
. . .the fish will grow double , triple ,
or quadruple its size . "

210
00:20:06,789 --> 00:20:10,042
lt occurred to me then that perhaps
the reason for my growth . . .

211
00:20:10,209 --> 00:20:12,753
. . .was that l was intended
for larger things.

212
00:20:12,920 --> 00:20:16,465
After all , a giant man can't have
an ordinary-sized life .

213
00:20:18,008 --> 00:20:21,303
As soon as my bones had settled
in their adult configuration . . .

214
00:20:21,470 --> 00:20:26,267
. . . l set upon my plan to make
a bigger place for myself in Ashton .

215
00:20:26,434 --> 00:20:27,685
Tigers, go!

216
00:20:43,159 --> 00:20:44,910
Edward Bloom !

217
00:21:27,703 --> 00:21:31,790
Doggy ! My doggy !
My doggy's trapped !

218
00:21:40,341 --> 00:21:44,053
l was the biggest thing
Ashton had ever seen .

219
00:21:44,845 --> 00:21:47,848
Until one day , a stranger arrived .

220
00:22:03,072 --> 00:22:07,743
Calm down . Calm down , everybody .
Calm down . That's enough .

221
00:22:07,910 --> 00:22:11,664
-Mr. Mayor, he ate an entire corn field .
-He ate my dog .

222
00:22:11,830 --> 00:22:14,416
If you ain't gonna stop him , mayor,
we will .

223
00:22:14,583 --> 00:22:17,670
I won't have mob violence
in this town .

224
00:22:17,837 --> 00:22:19,755
Now, has someone tried
talking to him?

225
00:22:19,922 --> 00:22:22,591
-You can't reason with him .
-He's a monster.

226
00:22:22,842 --> 00:22:24,552
I'll do it.

227
00:22:39,233 --> 00:22:41,569
I'll talk to him .
See if I can get him to go .

228
00:22:41,735 --> 00:22:44,113
That creature could crush you
without trying .

229
00:22:44,280 --> 00:22:47,283
Oh , trust me , he'll have to try .

230
00:23:18,814 --> 00:23:20,316
Hello?

231
00:23:24,361 --> 00:23:27,906
My name is Edward Bloom ,
and I wanna talk to you !

232
00:23:28,699 --> 00:23:31,076
Go away !

233
00:23:32,995 --> 00:23:36,040
Now, I'm not going anywhere
until you show yourself!

234
00:23:36,206 --> 00:23:39,209
I said , go away !

235
00:23:59,605 --> 00:24:02,024
Armed with the foreknowledge
of my own death . . .

236
00:24:02,191 --> 00:24:04,360
. . . l knew the giant couldn't kill me .

237
00:24:06,654 --> 00:24:09,949
All the same , l preferred to keep
my bones unbroken .

238
00:24:10,115 --> 00:24:12,117
Why are you here?

239
00:24:13,410 --> 00:24:14,912
So you can eat me .

240
00:24:15,371 --> 00:24:19,333
The town decided to send
a human sacrifice and I volunteered .

241
00:24:19,500 --> 00:24:23,087
My arms are a little stringy , but
there's some good eating in my legs .

242
00:24:23,253 --> 00:24:26,507
I mean , I'd be tempted
to eat them myself.

243
00:24:28,634 --> 00:24:30,719
So I guess, well . . . .

244
00:24:30,886 --> 00:24:35,182
If you'd just get it over with quick,
because I'm not much for pain , really .

245
00:24:47,653 --> 00:24:51,365
Oh , come on ! I can't go back !
I'm a human sacrifice !

246
00:24:51,532 --> 00:24:54,076
If I go back,
they'll think I'm a coward .

247
00:24:54,243 --> 00:24:56,829
I'd rather be dinner than a coward .

248
00:24:57,413 --> 00:24:58,664
Here .

249
00:24:59,164 --> 00:25:02,251
You can start with my hand .
It'll be an appetizer.

250
00:25:02,418 --> 00:25:06,588
I don't want to eat you .
I don't want to eat anybody .

251
00:25:06,755 --> 00:25:10,884
I just get so hungry .
I'm just too big .

252
00:25:12,052 --> 00:25:14,847
Did you ever think that
maybe you're not too big . . .

253
00:25:15,014 --> 00:25:17,141
. . . but maybe this town
is just too small?

254
00:25:17,307 --> 00:25:20,185
I've heard in real cities
there are buildings so tall . . .

255
00:25:20,352 --> 00:25:22,604
. . . you can't even see
the tops of them .

256
00:25:22,771 --> 00:25:25,274
-Really?
-Oh , I wouldn't lie to you .

257
00:25:25,441 --> 00:25:27,568
And all-you-can-eat buffets .

258
00:25:27,735 --> 00:25:30,738
-Now, you can eat a lot, can't you?
-I can .

259
00:25:30,904 --> 00:25:33,782
So why are you wasting your time
in a small town?

260
00:25:33,949 --> 00:25:37,077
You're a big man .
You should be in a big city .

261
00:25:37,244 --> 00:25:40,456
You're just trying to get me
to leave , aren't you?

262
00:25:40,748 --> 00:25:44,293
-What's your name , giant?
-Karl .

263
00:25:44,460 --> 00:25:47,880
Well , mine's Edward .
And truthfully . . . .

264
00:25:48,047 --> 00:25:51,884
Well , I do want you to leave , Karl .
But I want to leave with you .

265
00:25:52,050 --> 00:25:54,803
I mean , you think this town
is too small for you?

266
00:25:54,970 --> 00:25:58,098
Well , it's too small for a man
of my ambition .

267
00:25:58,265 --> 00:26:01,435
So , what do you say? Join me?

268
00:26:04,646 --> 00:26:05,856
Okay .

269
00:26:07,191 --> 00:26:08,692
Okay .

270
00:26:08,984 --> 00:26:12,154
Now, first, we gotta
get you ready for the city .

271
00:26:28,921 --> 00:26:33,175
Edward Bloom , first son of Ashton . . .

272
00:26:33,342 --> 00:26:36,845
. . . it's with a heavy heart
that we see you go .

273
00:26:37,012 --> 00:26:39,681
But take with you
this key to the city . . .

274
00:26:39,848 --> 00:26:42,810
. . . and know that any time
you want to come back. . .

275
00:26:42,976 --> 00:26:46,021
. . . all our doors are open to you .

276
00:26:54,530 --> 00:26:58,575
That afternoon as l left Ashton,
everyone seemed to have advice .

277
00:26:58,742 --> 00:27:01,620
Find yourself a nice girl , now!

278
00:27:02,037 --> 00:27:05,040
Watch your pride , Edward Bloom !

279
00:27:05,249 --> 00:27:09,628
But there was one person whose
counsel l held above all others.

280
00:27:13,715 --> 00:27:16,426
She said that the biggest fish
in the river. . .

281
00:27:16,593 --> 00:27:19,763
. . .gets that way
by never being caught.

282
00:27:24,476 --> 00:27:27,855
-What'd she say?
-Beats me .

283
00:27:39,700 --> 00:27:41,535
There were two roads out of Ashton :

284
00:27:41,702 --> 00:27:44,580
A new one which was paved
and an older one that wasn't.

285
00:27:44,746 --> 00:27:46,748
People didn't use
the old one anymore . . .

286
00:27:46,915 --> 00:27:49,751
. . . and it had developed a reputation
for being haunted .

287
00:27:49,918 --> 00:27:53,088
Well , since l had no intention
of ever returning to Ashton . . .

288
00:27:53,255 --> 00:27:57,426
. . .this seemed as good a time as any
to find out what lay down that oId one .

289
00:27:57,593 --> 00:28:02,347
-You know anyone who's taken it?
-That poet, Norther Winslow, did .

290
00:28:02,806 --> 00:28:05,267
He was going to Paris, France .

291
00:28:05,434 --> 00:28:08,896
I guess he liked it.
No one ever heard from him again .

292
00:28:09,104 --> 00:28:11,273
I'll tell you what.
You go the other way .

293
00:28:11,440 --> 00:28:14,985
I'll cut through here ,
and I'll meet you on the far side .

294
00:28:16,195 --> 00:28:19,281
You're just trying to run away ,
aren't you?

295
00:28:20,991 --> 00:28:22,242
Here .

296
00:28:23,535 --> 00:28:26,622
J ust to be sure , you can take my pack.

297
00:29:05,827 --> 00:29:07,788
Why , you son of a--

298
00:29:33,730 --> 00:29:36,358
Now, there comes a point
when a reasonable man . . .

299
00:29:36,525 --> 00:29:41,279
. . .will swallow his pride and admit
that he's made a terrible mistake.

300
00:29:41,446 --> 00:29:46,368
The truth is,
l was never a reasonable man .

301
00:29:47,536 --> 00:29:50,247
And what l recall of
Sunday school was that. . .

302
00:29:50,414 --> 00:29:55,544
. . .the more difficult something is,
the more rewarding it is in the end .

303
00:30:54,770 --> 00:30:57,564
Friend ! Welcome to you !

304
00:30:57,981 --> 00:31:00,984
-What's your name?
-Edward Bloom .

305
00:31:02,110 --> 00:31:05,197
-"Bloom" like a flower?
-Yes .

306
00:31:05,364 --> 00:31:09,534
Oh , here ! Here you are , right here .
"Edward Bloom . "

307
00:31:09,701 --> 00:31:12,037
We weren't e xpecting you yet.

308
00:31:12,204 --> 00:31:14,289
-You were e xpecting me?
-Not yet.

309
00:31:14,456 --> 00:31:19,127
-You must've taken a shortcut.
-Why , yes, I did . It almost killed me .

310
00:31:19,628 --> 00:31:23,799
Life will do that to you .
And truthfully , the long way is easier.

311
00:31:24,216 --> 00:31:27,094
-But it's longer.
-Much longer.

312
00:31:27,260 --> 00:31:30,180
And you're here now,
and that's what matters .

313
00:31:30,347 --> 00:31:31,598
What is this place?

314
00:31:31,765 --> 00:31:35,018
The town of Spectre .
Best-kept secret in Alabama !

315
00:31:35,185 --> 00:31:38,563
Now, it says here
you're from Ashton , right?

316
00:31:38,730 --> 00:31:42,067
Last person we had from Ashton
was Norther Winslow.

317
00:31:42,234 --> 00:31:44,152
The poet?
Whatever happened to him?

318
00:31:44,319 --> 00:31:48,698
He's still here . Let me buy you a drink.
I'll tell you all about it.

319
00:31:48,865 --> 00:31:50,826
Hell , I'll have him tell you !

320
00:31:50,992 --> 00:31:54,121
I have to meet somebody ,
and I'm already running late .

321
00:31:54,287 --> 00:31:58,166
Now, son , I already told you .
You're early .

322
00:32:32,701 --> 00:32:35,829
Now, tell me if that isn't
the best pie you ever ate .

323
00:32:35,996 --> 00:32:37,706
It truly is .

324
00:32:42,043 --> 00:32:45,964
Well , everything tastes better here .
Even the water is sweet.

325
00:32:48,467 --> 00:32:51,720
Never gets too hot,
too cold , too humid .

326
00:32:52,220 --> 00:32:55,432
At night, the wind goes through
the trees where you'd swear. . .

327
00:32:55,599 --> 00:32:58,977
. . .there was a whole symphony
out there playing just for you .

328
00:33:02,480 --> 00:33:03,982
Hey ! Jenny !

329
00:33:04,983 --> 00:33:06,776
Come back here !

330
00:33:12,490 --> 00:33:13,909
Jenny !

331
00:33:29,466 --> 00:33:31,259
Hey , I need those .

332
00:33:31,760 --> 00:33:35,096
-There is no softer ground than town .
-That almost rhymes .

333
00:33:35,263 --> 00:33:37,766
He's our poet laureate . Come on .

334
00:33:41,311 --> 00:33:44,814
l agreed to spend the afternoon ,
if only to understand . . .

335
00:33:44,981 --> 00:33:49,861
. . .the mystery of how a place could
feel so strange and yet so familiar.

336
00:33:53,782 --> 00:33:56,326
I've been working on this poem
for 1 2 years .

337
00:33:56,493 --> 00:33:58,119
Really?

338
00:33:58,286 --> 00:34:03,083
There's a lot of e xpectation .
I don't wanna disappoint my fans .

339
00:34:03,959 --> 00:34:05,627
May I?

340
00:34:25,897 --> 00:34:27,566
It's only three lines long .

341
00:34:30,902 --> 00:34:34,155
This is why you should never show
a work in progress .

342
00:35:29,836 --> 00:35:31,796
I got him !

343
00:35:47,395 --> 00:35:49,064
There's leeches in there .

344
00:35:50,023 --> 00:35:53,068
-Did you see that woman?
-What did she look like?

345
00:35:53,234 --> 00:35:56,696
-Well , she was . . . .
-Was she naked?

346
00:35:59,032 --> 00:36:00,575
Yes , she was .

347
00:36:00,742 --> 00:36:04,996
It's not a woman . It's a fish .
No one ever catches her.

348
00:36:05,163 --> 00:36:07,665
Fish looks different
to different people .

349
00:36:07,957 --> 00:36:11,753
My daddy said it looked like the
coon dog he had when he was a kid . . .

350
00:36:12,170 --> 00:36:14,673
. . . back from the dead .

351
00:36:18,093 --> 00:36:19,928
Oh , darn .

352
00:36:21,888 --> 00:36:24,099
-How old are you?
-Eighteen .

353
00:36:24,265 --> 00:36:28,186
I'm 8 . That means when I'm 1 8 ,
you'll be 28 .

354
00:36:28,353 --> 00:36:33,108
-And when I'm 28 , you'll only be 38 .
-You're pretty good at arithmetic.

355
00:36:33,274 --> 00:36:37,779
And when I'm 38 , you'll be 48 .
That's not much difference at all .

356
00:36:37,946 --> 00:36:40,782
Sure is a lot now, though , huh?

357
00:37:14,524 --> 00:37:17,152
Hey , Edward !

358
00:37:25,994 --> 00:37:30,081
Roses are red
Violets are blue

359
00:37:30,248 --> 00:37:32,792
l love Spectre . . . .

360
00:37:38,506 --> 00:37:40,508
E xcuse me .

361
00:37:46,681 --> 00:37:50,143
Jenny thinks you're quite a catch .
We all do .

362
00:37:50,977 --> 00:37:52,312
What?

363
00:37:52,479 --> 00:37:55,690
I said , you're quite a catch .

364
00:38:17,962 --> 00:38:19,714
I have to leave .

365
00:38:23,760 --> 00:38:25,136
Tonight.

366
00:38:25,595 --> 00:38:26,888
Why?

367
00:38:28,223 --> 00:38:30,767
This town is more than any man
could ask for.

368
00:38:30,934 --> 00:38:34,479
And if I were to end up here ,
I would consider myself lucky .

369
00:38:34,646 --> 00:38:38,566
But the truth is, I'm just not ready
to end up anywhere .

370
00:38:39,150 --> 00:38:40,860
But no one's ever left.

371
00:38:42,070 --> 00:38:45,406
How are you gonna make it
without your shoes?

372
00:38:45,573 --> 00:38:48,243
Well , I suspect it will hurt. A lot.

373
00:38:52,163 --> 00:38:54,833
Now, I'm sorry , but. . . .
Well , goodbye .

374
00:38:58,503 --> 00:39:02,173
-You won't find a better place .
-I don't e xpect to .

375
00:39:07,303 --> 00:39:09,347
Promise me you'll come back.

376
00:39:10,807 --> 00:39:15,603
I promise . Someday .
When I'm really supposed to .

377
00:39:46,593 --> 00:39:49,429
That night,
l reached two conclusions.

378
00:39:50,221 --> 00:39:55,268
The first was that a dangerous path
is made much worse by darkness.

379
00:39:55,435 --> 00:40:00,148
The second was that l was
hopelessly and irrevocably lost.

380
00:40:00,732 --> 00:40:03,735
These woods would become
my graveyard .

381
00:40:05,987 --> 00:40:10,867
As difficult as it was to reach Spectre ,
l was fated to get there eventuaIIy .

382
00:40:11,034 --> 00:40:14,454
After all , no man can avoid
reaching the end of his life .

383
00:40:29,093 --> 00:40:33,348
And then l realized
this wasn't the end of my life .

384
00:40:33,514 --> 00:40:36,517
This isn't how I die .

385
00:40:54,035 --> 00:40:55,370
Friend .

386
00:40:55,536 --> 00:40:59,457
-What happened to your shoes?
-They kind of got ahead of me .

387
00:41:16,391 --> 00:41:19,394
I don't know if you saw it,
but Josephine had some photos . . .

388
00:41:19,560 --> 00:41:21,145
. . . in the most recent Newsweek.

389
00:41:23,690 --> 00:41:25,191
Really?

390
00:41:25,358 --> 00:41:26,693
That's just wonderful .

391
00:41:28,528 --> 00:41:33,032
I spent a week in Morocco
for the story . It was incredible .

392
00:41:33,199 --> 00:41:37,662
Oh . We'll have to pick up a copy .

393
00:41:48,005 --> 00:41:51,092
I don't know if you're aware of this ,
Josephine . . .

394
00:41:51,551 --> 00:41:56,723
. . . but African parrots , in their native
Congo , they speak only French .

395
00:41:57,807 --> 00:41:59,100
Really?

396
00:41:59,684 --> 00:42:02,979
You're lucky to get four words
out of them in English .

397
00:42:03,146 --> 00:42:06,065
But if you were to walk
through the jungle . . .

398
00:42:06,232 --> 00:42:09,944
. . . you'd hear them speaking
the most elaborate French .

399
00:42:10,611 --> 00:42:15,783
Those parrots talk about everything .
Politics, movies, fashion .

400
00:42:15,950 --> 00:42:18,619
Everything but religion .

401
00:42:19,746 --> 00:42:21,080
Why not religion , Dad?

402
00:42:21,789 --> 00:42:26,461
It's rude to talk about religion . You
never know who you're gonna offend .

403
00:42:31,799 --> 00:42:34,969
Josephine actually went
to the Congo last year.

404
00:42:36,637 --> 00:42:38,681
Oh , so you know.

405
00:42:54,322 --> 00:42:56,783
Hello .

406
00:42:56,949 --> 00:42:59,327
H i . How are you feeling?

407
00:43:00,286 --> 00:43:03,623
Oh , I was dreaming .

408
00:43:04,165 --> 00:43:06,667
What were you dreaming about?

409
00:43:07,710 --> 00:43:10,755
Oh , I don't usually remember. . .

410
00:43:10,922 --> 00:43:15,259
. . . unless they're especially portentous .
Do you know what that word means?

411
00:43:15,551 --> 00:43:18,679
It means when you dream about
something that's gonna happen .

412
00:43:19,430 --> 00:43:21,933
Like one night, I had a dream . . .

413
00:43:22,100 --> 00:43:26,854
. . .where this crow came and said :

414
00:43:27,021 --> 00:43:29,524
"Your aunt is gonna die . "

415
00:43:30,691 --> 00:43:33,945
I was so scared ,
I woke up my parents . . .

416
00:43:34,111 --> 00:43:38,366
. . . but they said it was just a dream
and to get back to bed .

417
00:43:38,533 --> 00:43:42,036
But the ne xt morning ,
my Aunt Stacy was dead .

418
00:43:42,370 --> 00:43:44,372
That's terrible .

419
00:43:44,539 --> 00:43:49,669
Terrible for her, but think about me ,
young boy with that kind of power.

420
00:43:50,211 --> 00:43:54,966
Wasn't three weeks later when the
crow came back to me in a dream . . .

421
00:43:55,133 --> 00:43:59,846
. . . and said , "Your daddy's gonna die . "

422
00:44:00,680 --> 00:44:02,723
I didn't know what to do .

423
00:44:03,099 --> 00:44:06,769
I finally told my father, but he said :

424
00:44:06,936 --> 00:44:12,775
"Oh , not to worry . "
But I could see he was rattled .

425
00:44:12,942 --> 00:44:15,778
The ne xt morning , he wasn't himself.

426
00:44:15,945 --> 00:44:20,575
Kept looking around , waiting for
something to drop on his head .

427
00:44:20,741 --> 00:44:25,830
Because the crow didn't say how it
was gonna happen , just those words:

428
00:44:25,997 --> 00:44:28,249
"Your daddy's gonna die . "

429
00:44:28,833 --> 00:44:34,755
Well , he left home early
and was gone a long time .

430
00:44:34,922 --> 00:44:37,633
When he finally came back,
he looked terrible . . .

431
00:44:37,800 --> 00:44:41,095
. . . like he was waiting
for the a xe to fall all day .

432
00:44:42,096 --> 00:44:43,890
He said to my mother:

433
00:44:44,056 --> 00:44:47,810
"I've just had the worst day of my life . "

434
00:44:47,977 --> 00:44:51,981
"You think you've had a bad day?"
she said .

435
00:44:52,148 --> 00:44:57,278
"This morning , the milkman
dropped dead on the porch . "

436
00:44:59,447 --> 00:45:03,618
Because , see , my mother
was banging the milkman .

437
00:45:05,453 --> 00:45:10,124
-Can I take your picture?
-Oh , you don't need a picture .

438
00:45:10,291 --> 00:45:14,462
J ust look up the word "handsome"
in the dictionary .

439
00:45:14,629 --> 00:45:15,963
Please?

440
00:45:17,632 --> 00:45:19,091
All right.

441
00:45:22,470 --> 00:45:24,555
I have photos of the wedding
to show you .

442
00:45:24,722 --> 00:45:27,141
There is a great one
of you and my father.

443
00:45:33,481 --> 00:45:38,152
I want to see pictures of your wedding .
I've never seen any .

444
00:45:38,319 --> 00:45:41,739
That's because we didn't have
a proper wedding .

445
00:45:41,906 --> 00:45:45,326
Your mother-in-law was
never supposed to marry me .

446
00:45:45,493 --> 00:45:49,330
-She was engaged to somebody else .
-I never knew.

447
00:45:49,497 --> 00:45:52,083
Will never told you?

448
00:45:52,667 --> 00:45:55,127
Probably just as well .

449
00:45:55,294 --> 00:45:57,922
He would have told it wrong , anyway .

450
00:45:58,089 --> 00:46:01,300
All the facts , none of the flavour.

451
00:46:01,467 --> 00:46:05,137
Oh . So this is a tall tale .

452
00:46:06,013 --> 00:46:09,475
Well , it's not a short one .

453
00:46:12,144 --> 00:46:16,857
l'd just left Spectre and was
on my way to discover my destiny .

454
00:46:17,024 --> 00:46:19,819
Not knowing what
that would be exactly . . .

455
00:46:19,986 --> 00:46:24,907
. . . l ex plored every opportunity
that presented itself.

456
00:46:53,769 --> 00:46:58,065
Coco ! Coco ! There they are .

457
00:46:59,316 --> 00:47:02,737
Ladies and gentlemen !

458
00:47:02,903 --> 00:47:06,657
You may think
you've seen the unusual .

459
00:47:07,491 --> 00:47:11,829
You may think you've seen the bizarre .

460
00:47:13,164 --> 00:47:18,002
But I've travelled
to the five corners of the world . . .

461
00:47:18,377 --> 00:47:24,884
. . . and let me tell you ,
I've never seen anything like this !

462
00:47:30,306 --> 00:47:32,349
When I met this man . . .

463
00:47:32,516 --> 00:47:35,227
. . . he was picking oranges in Florida.

464
00:47:35,394 --> 00:47:39,440
H is fellow workers called him
El Penumbra. "The Shadow. "

465
00:47:41,400 --> 00:47:43,778
Because when you worked
beside him . . .

466
00:47:44,320 --> 00:47:46,614
. . . he blocked out the daylight!

467
00:47:47,281 --> 00:47:51,368
Not to alarm you , ma'am ,
but if this man wanted to . . .

468
00:47:51,535 --> 00:47:55,998
. . . he could crush your head
between his toes . . .

469
00:47:56,165 --> 00:47:59,710
. . . like a tiny walnut.
But he won't do it.

470
00:47:59,877 --> 00:48:02,463
No . No , ladies and gentlemen . . .

471
00:48:02,630 --> 00:48:04,757
. . . he will not hurt her. . .

472
00:48:05,049 --> 00:48:08,636
. . . because he is our own gentle giant.

473
00:48:09,970 --> 00:48:16,143
Ladies and gentlemen ,
I give you Colossus !

474
00:49:40,686 --> 00:49:44,315
Ladies and gentlemen ,
boys and girls . . .

475
00:49:44,481 --> 00:49:46,859
. . .thank y'all for coming .

476
00:49:47,026 --> 00:49:50,571
Drive home safely , everyone .
Thank you for coming .

477
00:49:51,071 --> 00:49:54,742
lt was on that night
Karl met his destiny .

478
00:49:55,034 --> 00:49:58,412
And l met mine , almost.

479
00:49:58,579 --> 00:50:03,417
They say when you meet
the love of your life , time stops.

480
00:50:03,584 --> 00:50:05,085
And that's true.

481
00:50:50,297 --> 00:50:53,425
What they don't tell you
is that once time starts again . . .

482
00:50:54,134 --> 00:50:57,930
. . . it moves extra fast to catch up .

483
00:51:30,796 --> 00:51:32,172
What's your name?

484
00:51:33,090 --> 00:51:37,386
-It's Karl .
-Karl .

485
00:51:42,850 --> 00:51:48,397
Tell me , Karl , have you ever heard
the term "involuntary servitude"?

486
00:51:48,564 --> 00:51:51,442
-No .
-"U nconscionable contract"?

487
00:51:53,610 --> 00:51:56,488
-Nope .
-Great.

488
00:51:56,655 --> 00:51:58,699
Here you go .

489
00:51:59,867 --> 00:52:01,535
Here you are .

490
00:52:04,788 --> 00:52:07,541
Mr. Soggybottom ,
come down this here . . .

491
00:52:07,708 --> 00:52:10,044
. . . so he can use your back
to sign on it.

492
00:52:22,181 --> 00:52:25,059
Okay . Thank you . Yeah .

493
00:52:26,352 --> 00:52:28,270
Hey , kid .

494
00:52:28,437 --> 00:52:31,815
-Your friend just made himself a star.
-Oh , that's great.

495
00:52:34,109 --> 00:52:36,779
-My attorney , Mr. Soggybottom .
-Good to meet you .

496
00:52:36,945 --> 00:52:38,614
Pleased to meet you .

497
00:52:43,744 --> 00:52:46,830
What's the matter? I haven't
seen a customer so depressed . . .

498
00:52:46,997 --> 00:52:50,250
. . . since the elephant sat
on that farmer's wife .

499
00:52:51,502 --> 00:52:53,670
"Depressed"?

500
00:52:55,255 --> 00:52:57,716
See? The big guy likes it.

501
00:52:58,509 --> 00:53:00,594
I just saw the woman I'm gonna marry .

502
00:53:00,761 --> 00:53:03,889
I know it. But I lost her.

503
00:53:04,973 --> 00:53:06,308
Oh , tough break.

504
00:53:06,475 --> 00:53:10,354
Well , most men have to get married
before they lose their wives .

505
00:53:10,521 --> 00:53:15,609
I'm gonna spend the rest of my life
looking for her. That or die alone .

506
00:53:15,984 --> 00:53:17,861
Damn , kid .

507
00:53:21,115 --> 00:53:23,450
Let me guess . Real pretty?

508
00:53:24,785 --> 00:53:26,620
Reddish-blondish hair?

509
00:53:28,414 --> 00:53:29,790
Blue dress?

510
00:53:31,125 --> 00:53:32,793
Yeah !

511
00:53:32,960 --> 00:53:35,963
I know her uncle . Friends of the family .

512
00:53:36,130 --> 00:53:38,257
Who is she? Where does she live?

513
00:53:38,424 --> 00:53:41,301
Forget it. Don't waste your time .
She's out of your league .

514
00:53:41,468 --> 00:53:44,972
-What? You don't even know me .
-Sure I do .

515
00:53:45,139 --> 00:53:49,601
You were hot shit back in H ickville , but
here in the real world , you got squat.

516
00:53:49,768 --> 00:53:51,687
You don't have a plan or a job .

517
00:53:51,854 --> 00:53:54,314
Nothing e xcept the clothes
on your back.

518
00:53:54,815 --> 00:53:57,693
Well , I have a whole backpack
full of clothes .

519
00:53:58,819 --> 00:54:01,613
Oh , someone stole my backpack.

520
00:54:02,364 --> 00:54:06,326
You were a big fish in a small pond ,
but this here is the ocean . . .

521
00:54:06,493 --> 00:54:09,079
. . . and you're drowning .
Go back to Puddleville .

522
00:54:09,246 --> 00:54:12,666
-You'll be happy there .
-You say I don't have a plan . I do .

523
00:54:12,833 --> 00:54:16,462
I'll find that girl , marry her,
and spend the rest of my life with her.

524
00:54:17,004 --> 00:54:20,716
I don't have a job , but I would
have a job if you gave me one .

525
00:54:20,883 --> 00:54:22,926
And I may not have much . . .

526
00:54:23,093 --> 00:54:26,805
. . . but I have more determination
than any man you're likely to meet.

527
00:54:29,016 --> 00:54:32,686
I'm sorry , kid . I don't do charity .
Come on , big boy .

528
00:54:33,687 --> 00:54:36,064
Wait. Look, I'll work
night and day for you . . .

529
00:54:36,231 --> 00:54:38,025
. . . and you won't have to pay me .

530
00:54:40,027 --> 00:54:43,030
You just have to tell me who she is .

531
00:54:46,533 --> 00:54:48,535
Every month you work for me . . .

532
00:54:48,702 --> 00:54:52,122
. . . I'll tell you one thing about her.
That's my final offer.

533
00:54:56,043 --> 00:54:58,295
Let's get started .

534
00:55:11,350 --> 00:55:15,354
From that moment on , l did everything
Mr. Calloway asked .

535
00:55:15,646 --> 00:55:18,607
l'd go three days
without stopping to eat.

536
00:55:18,774 --> 00:55:21,401
And four days without sleeping .

537
00:55:21,568 --> 00:55:24,446
What kept me going was
the promise of meeting the girl . . .

538
00:55:24,613 --> 00:55:25,906
. . .who would be my wife .

539
00:55:33,580 --> 00:55:36,041
Stick together, that's the way
it's gonna be .

540
00:55:36,208 --> 00:55:40,087
-The way it was, and the way it will be .
-Mr. Calloway , sir?

541
00:55:40,254 --> 00:55:42,047
It's been a month today .

542
00:55:43,966 --> 00:55:47,219
This girl , the love of your life . . .

543
00:55:47,386 --> 00:55:52,349
. . . her favourite flowers are daffodils.

544
00:55:53,350 --> 00:55:57,729
-So get that stuff. . . .
-Daffodils .

545
00:55:58,438 --> 00:56:00,858
Daffodils .

546
00:56:02,442 --> 00:56:04,695
Daffodils .

547
00:56:10,784 --> 00:56:12,327
True to his word . . .

548
00:56:12,494 --> 00:56:15,289
. . .every month Amos would tell me
something new . . .

549
00:56:15,455 --> 00:56:17,791
. . . about the woman of my dreams.

550
00:56:17,958 --> 00:56:20,919
Enjoy yourselves , enjoy yourselves .

551
00:56:21,086 --> 00:56:22,588
She's going to college .

552
00:56:22,963 --> 00:56:27,259
College . She's going to college .

553
00:56:37,811 --> 00:56:39,271
She likes music.

554
00:56:39,980 --> 00:56:43,734
Music. She likes music.

555
00:56:47,988 --> 00:56:52,284
Over the months, l learned a lot
about the woman l would marry . . .

556
00:56:52,451 --> 00:56:55,787
. . . but not her name ,
and not where to find her.

557
00:56:55,954 --> 00:57:00,709
That time had come .
l couldn't wait any longer.

558
00:57:12,012 --> 00:57:14,306
Mr. Calloway?

559
00:57:16,350 --> 00:57:19,645
It's Edward Bloom .
I need to talk to you .

560
00:57:23,899 --> 00:57:26,318
Mr. Calloway?

561
00:58:00,102 --> 00:58:01,478
No . Wait!

562
00:58:19,579 --> 00:58:24,209
That night, l discovered that most
things you consider evil or wicked . . .

563
00:58:24,376 --> 00:58:28,255
. . . are simply lonely and lacking
in social niceties.

564
00:59:02,664 --> 00:59:04,791
Didn't kill anything , did I?

565
00:59:05,751 --> 00:59:10,213
A few rabbits. But I think one of them
was already dead .

566
00:59:13,425 --> 00:59:16,470
That would e xplain the indigestion .

567
00:59:23,560 --> 00:59:25,562
Thank you .

568
00:59:27,564 --> 00:59:29,733
I was wrong about you , kid .

569
00:59:29,900 --> 00:59:33,320
You may not have much ,
but what you got, you got a lot of.

570
00:59:33,653 --> 00:59:36,114
You could get any girl .

571
00:59:36,281 --> 00:59:38,283
There's only one I want.

572
00:59:42,996 --> 00:59:44,331
Her name . . .

573
00:59:47,667 --> 00:59:49,836
. . . is Sandra Templeton .

574
00:59:51,922 --> 00:59:54,591
She goes to Auburn .

575
00:59:55,008 --> 00:59:59,346
Semester's almost over,
so you better hurry .

576
00:59:59,513 --> 01:00:01,264
Thank you . Thank you .

577
01:00:01,848 --> 01:00:04,351
-Good luck, kid !
-Thank you , sir!

578
01:00:16,863 --> 01:00:19,491
-Bye , now.
-Bye , Edward .

579
01:00:20,867 --> 01:00:24,287
After saying my goodbyes,
l hopped three trains. . .

580
01:00:24,454 --> 01:00:27,874
. . .to get to Auburn that afternoon .

581
01:01:03,034 --> 01:01:06,705
You don't know me ,
but my name is Edward Bloom . . .

582
01:01:06,872 --> 01:01:08,248
. . . and I love you .

583
01:01:10,125 --> 01:01:13,879
I've spent the last three years
working to find out who you are .

584
01:01:14,045 --> 01:01:18,341
I've been shot, stabbed , and trampled
a few times . I broke my ribs twice .

585
01:01:18,800 --> 01:01:21,845
But it's all been worth it
to see you here now. . .

586
01:01:22,012 --> 01:01:24,973
. . . and to finally get to talk to you .

587
01:01:25,140 --> 01:01:27,517
Because I'm destined to marry you .

588
01:01:28,393 --> 01:01:33,732
I knew it when I saw you at the circus ,
and I know it now more than ever.

589
01:01:35,901 --> 01:01:39,279
-I'm sorry .
-You don't have to apologise to me .

590
01:01:39,446 --> 01:01:41,781
I'm the luckiest person
you'll find today .

591
01:01:41,948 --> 01:01:45,118
No , I'm sorry I'm engaged
to be married .

592
01:01:52,292 --> 01:01:55,462
But you're wrong . I do know you .

593
01:01:55,629 --> 01:01:57,589
At least by reputation .

594
01:01:58,506 --> 01:02:00,926
Edward Bloom from Ashton .

595
01:02:01,468 --> 01:02:05,805
See , I'm actually engaged
to a boy from Ashton . Don Price .

596
01:02:06,514 --> 01:02:09,059
He was a few years older than you .

597
01:02:19,694 --> 01:02:21,571
Well . . . .

598
01:02:23,698 --> 01:02:25,241
Congratulations.

599
01:02:28,495 --> 01:02:30,997
I'm sorry to have bothered you .

600
01:02:35,961 --> 01:02:39,047
Stop it! It's not funny .

601
01:02:39,756 --> 01:02:42,217
That poor boy .

602
01:02:42,842 --> 01:02:46,012
Fate has a cruel way
of circling around on you .

603
01:02:46,179 --> 01:02:48,265
After all this work to leave Ashton . . .

604
01:02:48,431 --> 01:02:53,895
. . .the girl l love was now engaged
to one of its biggest jerks.

605
01:02:54,062 --> 01:02:56,314
There's a time when a man
needs to fight. . .

606
01:02:56,481 --> 01:02:59,943
. . . and a time when he needs to accept
that his destiny's lost. . .

607
01:03:00,110 --> 01:03:04,197
. . .the ship has sailed,
and that only a fool will continue .

608
01:03:04,364 --> 01:03:07,701
Truth is, l've always been a fool .

609
01:03:08,702 --> 01:03:13,665
Sandra Templeton , I love you ,
and I will marry you !

610
01:03:18,003 --> 01:03:21,381
So as you can see , if we apply
these rules to our everyday life . . .

611
01:03:22,048 --> 01:03:24,801
. . . supply and demand
makes much more sense .

612
01:03:24,968 --> 01:03:29,014
Take a look at the ne xt graph , and the
import of this will be even stronger--

613
01:03:38,064 --> 01:03:40,734
--for three hours .

614
01:03:40,900 --> 01:03:42,777
Look !

615
01:04:13,308 --> 01:04:16,603
-Daffodils !
-They're your favourite flower.

616
01:04:16,770 --> 01:04:18,188
How did you get so many?

617
01:04:18,897 --> 01:04:21,066
I called everywhere in five states .

618
01:04:21,232 --> 01:04:24,194
I told them it was the only way
to get my wife to marry me .

619
01:04:26,404 --> 01:04:28,114
You don't even know me .

620
01:04:28,740 --> 01:04:31,201
I have the rest of my life to find out.

621
01:04:32,327 --> 01:04:33,995
Sandra !

622
01:04:35,872 --> 01:04:38,458
It's Don . Promise you won't hurt him .

623
01:04:38,625 --> 01:04:41,544
If that's what you want, I swear to it.

624
01:04:41,961 --> 01:04:44,422
-Bloom?
-Don .

625
01:04:45,215 --> 01:04:49,552
What the hell are you doing?
This is my girl . Mine !

626
01:04:49,719 --> 01:04:52,305
I wasn't aware
that she belonged to anyone .

627
01:04:55,600 --> 01:04:58,603
What's the matter?
Are you too scared to fight back?

628
01:04:58,770 --> 01:05:00,438
I promised I wouldn't.

629
01:05:01,648 --> 01:05:03,942
Stop it!

630
01:05:04,442 --> 01:05:05,735
Don , stop !

631
01:05:05,902 --> 01:05:11,324
While l took the beating of a lifetime ,
Don Price was ultimately defeated .

632
01:05:11,491 --> 01:05:16,246
All the physical activity had worsened
a congenital valve defect.

633
01:05:16,413 --> 01:05:18,581
Put simply , his heart
wasn't strong enough .

634
01:05:31,177 --> 01:05:33,638
Don ! I will never marry you .

635
01:05:34,514 --> 01:05:37,225
What?
You mean , you love this guy?

636
01:05:37,433 --> 01:05:40,562
He's almost a stranger,
and I prefer him to you .

637
01:06:08,756 --> 01:06:14,137
As it turned out, Sandra was able to
keep her same date at the chapel .

638
01:06:14,304 --> 01:06:17,515
Only the groom had changed .

639
01:06:18,475 --> 01:06:22,520
I thought you said you didn't
have a church wedding .

640
01:06:22,687 --> 01:06:25,773
We were all set to ,
but there was a complication .

641
01:06:29,611 --> 01:06:33,364
Is it the medicine
that's making you thirsty?

642
01:06:33,948 --> 01:06:37,494
Truth is ,
I've been thirsty my whole life .

643
01:06:38,620 --> 01:06:41,164
Never really known why .

644
01:06:41,956 --> 01:06:44,584
There was a time when I was 1 1 . . . .

645
01:06:44,751 --> 01:06:46,711
You were talking about your wedding .

646
01:06:46,878 --> 01:06:50,798
I didn't forget.
I was just working on a tangent.

647
01:06:50,965 --> 01:06:54,636
You see , most men , they'll tell you
a story straight through .

648
01:06:54,802 --> 01:06:58,848
It won't be complicated ,
but it won't be interesting , either.

649
01:06:59,015 --> 01:07:00,683
I like your stories .

650
01:07:00,850 --> 01:07:02,477
And I like you .

651
01:07:02,644 --> 01:07:07,106
Well , when you work for the circus,
you don't have a regular address .

652
01:07:07,273 --> 01:07:10,443
So after three years,
there was a lot of undelivered mail .

653
01:07:11,402 --> 01:07:14,113
During the four weeks
I was in the hospital . . .

654
01:07:14,280 --> 01:07:16,950
. . .the postmaster
finally caught up with me .

655
01:07:17,116 --> 01:07:20,620
And it seems that while my heart
belonged to Sandra. . .

656
01:07:20,787 --> 01:07:26,459
. . .the rest of my body belonged
to the U . S . government.

657
01:07:43,434 --> 01:07:47,105
A hitch in the Army was up to
three years at that point. . .

658
01:07:47,272 --> 01:07:50,316
. . . and having waited three years
just to meet Sandra. . .

659
01:07:50,483 --> 01:07:54,445
. . . I knew I couldn't survive
being away from her that long .

660
01:07:54,612 --> 01:07:57,740
So I took every hazardous
assignment I could find . . .

661
01:07:57,907 --> 01:08:02,245
. . .with the hope of getting
my time down to less than a year.

662
01:08:02,412 --> 01:08:04,956
Go ! Go !

663
01:08:07,500 --> 01:08:09,585
When I was offered
a secret mission . . .

664
01:08:09,752 --> 01:08:13,715
. . .to steal the plans for
the Wong Kai Tang power plant. . .

665
01:08:13,881 --> 01:08:17,010
. . . I jumped at the chance
to serve my country .

666
01:08:20,096 --> 01:08:22,682
Go ! Go !

667
01:10:18,005 --> 01:10:20,466
Any of you got needs?

668
01:12:06,405 --> 01:12:08,449
How could you miss your cue?

669
01:12:08,616 --> 01:12:12,578
You make me look like a fool
out there alone .

670
01:12:12,912 --> 01:12:14,455
You weren't alone !

671
01:12:16,624 --> 01:12:17,834
Who the hell are you?

672
01:12:18,000 --> 01:12:19,836
I'm not going to hurt you .

673
01:12:20,002 --> 01:12:21,003
Damn right you're not.

674
01:12:21,170 --> 01:12:22,171
G UARD !

675
01:12:24,006 --> 01:12:25,675
Tell your men not to bother us !

676
01:12:25,841 --> 01:12:27,134
And close that curtain !

677
01:12:29,887 --> 01:12:32,014
Please , I need your help .

678
01:12:32,640 --> 01:12:35,142
What makes you think we'll help you?

679
01:12:43,776 --> 01:12:48,865
Over the next hour, l described
my love for Sandra K. Templeton . . .

680
01:12:49,031 --> 01:12:51,951
. . . and the ordeal that brought me
before them.

681
01:12:52,118 --> 01:12:55,454
As it had always been ,
this love was my salvation .

682
01:12:55,621 --> 01:12:57,790
lt was destined to be.

683
01:12:57,957 --> 01:13:01,127
We put together an elaborate
plan for escape . . .

684
01:13:01,294 --> 01:13:03,546
. . . involving a whaling ship
to Russia . . .

685
01:13:03,713 --> 01:13:07,800
. . . a barge to Cuba,
and a small , dirty canoe to Miami .

686
01:13:07,967 --> 01:13:10,469
We all knew it would be dangerous.

687
01:13:11,679 --> 01:13:14,140
And what are we supposed to do
when we get to America?

688
01:13:14,932 --> 01:13:16,225
I can get you bookings .

689
01:13:16,392 --> 01:13:19,145
I know the biggest man
in show business .

690
01:13:20,271 --> 01:13:21,314
Bob Hope? !

691
01:13:21,647 --> 01:13:22,648
Bigger.

692
01:13:26,444 --> 01:13:27,445
Ready?

693
01:13:30,823 --> 01:13:34,702
And so the twins and l
began our arduous journey . . .

694
01:13:34,869 --> 01:13:36,829
. . . halfway around the world .

695
01:13:36,996 --> 01:13:41,334
Unfortunately , there was no way
to send a message back to America .

696
01:13:41,500 --> 01:13:45,504
And so it was no surprise
that the Army believed l was dead .

697
01:14:01,771 --> 01:14:04,315
No , God !

698
01:14:05,941 --> 01:14:10,321
After four months, Sandra had gotten
over the worst of the nightmares.

699
01:14:10,488 --> 01:14:14,909
When the phone rang, she didn't think
it was somehow me calling her.

700
01:14:15,076 --> 01:14:19,914
When a car drove past, she didn't
get up to check out the window .

701
01:14:54,990 --> 01:14:58,077
I talked with your father last night.

702
01:15:01,580 --> 01:15:04,583
You never told me
how your parents met.

703
01:15:04,750 --> 01:15:06,585
They met at Auburn .

704
01:15:06,752 --> 01:15:10,589
What about the details?
How they fell in love .

705
01:15:10,756 --> 01:15:12,717
The circus. The war.

706
01:15:12,883 --> 01:15:14,760
You never told me any of that.

707
01:15:14,927 --> 01:15:17,096
That's because most of it
never happened .

708
01:15:18,931 --> 01:15:21,183
But it's romantic.

709
01:15:24,603 --> 01:15:27,148
-What?
-What, I know better. . .

710
01:15:27,314 --> 01:15:30,484
. . .than to argue romance
with a French woman .

711
01:15:32,236 --> 01:15:34,238
Do you love your father?

712
01:15:34,405 --> 01:15:37,992
Everyone loves my father.
He's a very likeable guy .

713
01:15:39,368 --> 01:15:40,953
Do you love him?

714
01:15:46,083 --> 01:15:48,085
You have to understand .

715
01:15:48,252 --> 01:15:52,006
When I was growing up ,
he was gone more than he was there .

716
01:15:52,173 --> 01:15:56,135
And I started thinking maybe he's got
this second life somewhere else.

717
01:15:57,052 --> 01:15:59,805
Another family , another house .

718
01:15:59,972 --> 01:16:03,267
And he leaves us
and goes to them .

719
01:16:03,434 --> 01:16:05,061
Or, or. . . .

720
01:16:07,480 --> 01:16:11,567
Or maybe there is no second family .
Maybe he never wanted a family .

721
01:16:12,610 --> 01:16:17,198
Whatever it is, he likes his second life
better and he tells his stories . . .

722
01:16:17,364 --> 01:16:19,867
. . . because he can't stand
this boring place .

723
01:16:20,034 --> 01:16:23,162
-But it's not true .
-Well , what's true?

724
01:16:25,206 --> 01:16:28,000
He's never told me
a single true thing .

725
01:16:29,960 --> 01:16:31,504
Look, hey . . . .

726
01:16:32,880 --> 01:16:35,674
I know why you like him .

727
01:16:35,841 --> 01:16:38,636
I know why everyone likes him .

728
01:16:39,762 --> 01:16:43,057
But I need you to tell me
that I'm not crazy .

729
01:16:43,224 --> 01:16:46,143
Oh , you're not.

730
01:16:48,145 --> 01:16:50,856
And I think you should talk to him .

731
01:16:56,028 --> 01:16:59,198
Larry Puckett's Chevrolet
lets the customer do the talking .

732
01:16:59,365 --> 01:17:03,035
Larry Puckett's G M-certified vehicles
are under factory warranty .

733
01:17:03,202 --> 01:17:07,248
And you can save up to 40 percent
off the original M SRP .

734
01:17:07,414 --> 01:17:10,126
-Did I ever tell you about--?
-Yes .

735
01:17:11,377 --> 01:17:14,797
The maple tree and the Buick?
We've heard it.

736
01:17:17,216 --> 01:17:20,719
I know someone who hasn't.

737
01:17:20,886 --> 01:17:24,557
-The--
-Tree fell on the car, spilling the syrup .

738
01:17:24,723 --> 01:17:28,227
Which attracted the flies,
which got stuck to it. . .

739
01:17:28,394 --> 01:17:31,480
. . . and flew off with the whole car.

740
01:17:35,985 --> 01:17:39,446
But the real story
is how I got the car.

741
01:17:39,613 --> 01:17:41,323
-You see--
-Dad?

742
01:17:41,490 --> 01:17:42,950
Son .

743
01:17:43,117 --> 01:17:45,161
Can we talk?

744
01:17:47,580 --> 01:17:50,040
I think I'll get started
on these dishes .

745
01:17:50,207 --> 01:17:52,418
I'll help you .

746
01:18:07,933 --> 01:18:11,270
-You know about icebergs, Dad?
-Do I?

747
01:18:11,437 --> 01:18:13,898
I saw an iceberg once .

748
01:18:14,064 --> 01:18:17,359
They were hauling it down to Te xas
for drinking water.

749
01:18:17,526 --> 01:18:21,614
They didn't count on there being
an elephant frozen inside .

750
01:18:21,780 --> 01:18:24,575
-The woolly kind . A mammoth .
-Dad !

751
01:18:24,742 --> 01:18:26,702
What?

752
01:18:28,495 --> 01:18:30,706
I'm trying to make a metaphor here .

753
01:18:30,873 --> 01:18:33,584
Well , you shouldn't have started
with a question . . .

754
01:18:33,751 --> 01:18:36,045
. . . because people
want to answer questions .

755
01:18:36,211 --> 01:18:39,798
You should've started with ,
"The thing about icebergs is. . . . "

756
01:18:39,965 --> 01:18:43,427
Okay , okay . The thing about
icebergs is you only see 1 0 percent.

757
01:18:43,594 --> 01:18:46,972
The other 90 percent is below
the water where you can't see it.

758
01:18:47,139 --> 01:18:49,225
And that's . . .

759
01:18:49,391 --> 01:18:52,144
. . .what it is with you , Dad .

760
01:18:52,311 --> 01:18:55,064
I am only seeing this little bit. . .

761
01:18:55,230 --> 01:18:57,066
. . .that sticks above the water.

762
01:18:57,232 --> 01:19:00,402
Oh , you're only seeing down
to my nose? My chin? My--

763
01:19:00,569 --> 01:19:03,489
Dad , I have no idea who you are . . .

764
01:19:03,656 --> 01:19:06,909
. . . because you've never told me
a single fact.

765
01:19:07,076 --> 01:19:11,330
I've told you a thousand facts, Will .
That's what I do , I tell stories !

766
01:19:11,497 --> 01:19:14,750
You tell . . . lies, Dad .

767
01:19:14,917 --> 01:19:16,252
You tell amusing lies .

768
01:19:16,418 --> 01:19:19,505
Stories are what you tell
a 5-year-old at bedtime .

769
01:19:19,672 --> 01:19:22,549
They're not elaborate mythologies
that you maintain . . .

770
01:19:22,716 --> 01:19:25,177
. . .when your son is 1 0 and 1 5
and 20 and 30 .

771
01:19:25,344 --> 01:19:28,180
And I believed you .

772
01:19:29,974 --> 01:19:33,394
I believed your stories
so much longer than I should have .

773
01:19:33,560 --> 01:19:36,689
Then when I realized
everything you said was impossible . . .

774
01:19:36,855 --> 01:19:39,066
. . . I felt like a fool
to have trusted you .

775
01:19:39,233 --> 01:19:41,527
You're like Santa Claus
and the Easter Bunny .

776
01:19:41,694 --> 01:19:43,529
J ust as charming and just as fake .

777
01:19:43,696 --> 01:19:45,906
You think I'm fake .

778
01:19:47,366 --> 01:19:51,537
Only on the surface , Dad .
But it's all I've ever seen .

779
01:19:53,289 --> 01:19:54,957
Look.

780
01:19:56,542 --> 01:19:58,961
I'm about to have . . .

781
01:19:59,461 --> 01:20:00,713
. . . a kid of my own .

782
01:20:00,879 --> 01:20:03,549
It would kill me
if he went through his whole life . . .

783
01:20:03,716 --> 01:20:05,801
. . . never understanding me .

784
01:20:05,968 --> 01:20:08,387
It would kill you , huh?

785
01:20:10,389 --> 01:20:13,892
What do you want, Will?
Who do you want me to be?

786
01:20:14,059 --> 01:20:15,728
J ust yourself.

787
01:20:15,894 --> 01:20:19,732
Good , bad , everything .
J ust show me who you are for once .

788
01:20:19,898 --> 01:20:22,985
I've been nothing but myself
since the day I was born .

789
01:20:23,152 --> 01:20:27,698
And if you can't see that,
it's your failing , not mine !

790
01:20:59,021 --> 01:21:03,609
Your father decided
that he needed to have an office . . .

791
01:21:03,776 --> 01:21:06,862
. . . and , of course , it wouldn't do
to have it in the house .

792
01:21:07,029 --> 01:21:08,572
So . . . .

793
01:21:10,199 --> 01:21:13,952
You'll know better than me
what's important.

794
01:21:25,255 --> 01:21:27,383
What is it?

795
01:21:28,801 --> 01:21:30,803
It was during the war.

796
01:21:30,969 --> 01:21:34,848
Your father went missing .
They thought he was dead .

797
01:21:36,058 --> 01:21:38,477
Oh , that really happened?

798
01:21:38,644 --> 01:21:42,398
Not everything your father says
is a complete fabrication .

799
01:21:43,732 --> 01:21:46,777
I think I'll go check on him .

800
01:21:48,946 --> 01:21:51,615
I need to lie down for a bit.

801
01:21:51,782 --> 01:21:54,409
Okay , go .

802
01:22:09,174 --> 01:22:13,971
After the war, the sons of Alabama
returned home , looking for work.

803
01:22:14,138 --> 01:22:15,889
Each had the advantage over me .

804
01:22:16,056 --> 01:22:19,643
They were alive ,
while l was officially deceased .

805
01:22:19,810 --> 01:22:21,562
With my prospects few . . .

806
01:22:21,728 --> 01:22:25,524
. . . l took a job as a travelling salesman .
lt suited me.

807
01:22:25,691 --> 01:22:28,861
lf there was one thing
you can say about Edward Bloom . . .

808
01:22:29,027 --> 01:22:31,238
. . . it's that l am a social person .

809
01:22:31,947 --> 01:22:35,367
-Congratulations.
-Thank you , sir.

810
01:22:36,034 --> 01:22:38,620
l could be gone
for weeks at a time.

811
01:22:38,787 --> 01:22:41,707
But every other Friday,
l put all the money l made . . .

812
01:22:41,874 --> 01:22:45,544
. . . into an account set aside
for a proper house . . .

813
01:22:45,711 --> 01:22:47,880
. . .with a white picket fence .

814
01:22:53,677 --> 01:22:57,222
I'd like a moment of your time
to tell you about my new product. . .

815
01:22:57,389 --> 01:22:59,725
. . .the Handi-matic.

816
01:23:15,199 --> 01:23:18,493
A few years later, l added
other products and other cities. . .

817
01:23:18,660 --> 01:23:22,247
. . . until my territory stretched from
the coast to western Texas.

818
01:23:47,105 --> 01:23:48,607
Edward?

819
01:23:48,774 --> 01:23:50,400
Edward Bloom !

820
01:23:50,567 --> 01:23:52,194
It's me , Norther Winslow.

821
01:23:52,361 --> 01:23:54,238
I don't believe it.

822
01:23:54,404 --> 01:23:58,700
l was astonished to see the greatest
poet of both Ashton and Spectre . . .

823
01:23:58,867 --> 01:24:00,786
. . . all the way out in Texas.

824
01:24:00,953 --> 01:24:04,790
I want you to know, when you left
Spectre , it opened my eyes.

825
01:24:04,957 --> 01:24:08,627
There was a whole life out there
that I was not living .

826
01:24:08,794 --> 01:24:10,295
So I travelled .

827
01:24:10,462 --> 01:24:13,632
I saw France , Africa,
half of South America.

828
01:24:13,799 --> 01:24:16,468
Every day , a new adventure .
That's my motto .

829
01:24:16,635 --> 01:24:18,804
That's great, Norther.
I'm happy for you .

830
01:24:18,971 --> 01:24:21,223
What are you doing now?

831
01:24:22,307 --> 01:24:23,976
I'm robbing this place .

832
01:24:27,980 --> 01:24:29,982
All right, everybody down !

833
01:24:31,441 --> 01:24:34,695
Hey !
J ust slide that over.

834
01:24:40,909 --> 01:24:43,662
-Would you mind grabbing that?
-What?

835
01:24:44,121 --> 01:24:45,622
The gun .

836
01:24:47,124 --> 01:24:49,543
I'm gonna be cleaning out
the cash drawers . . .

837
01:24:49,710 --> 01:24:53,922
. . . and my associate here
is gonna handle the vault.

838
01:24:59,928 --> 01:25:03,390
All right, you !
You help my friend , okay?

839
01:25:04,182 --> 01:25:05,851
Let's go !

840
01:25:25,537 --> 01:25:27,456
I'm sorry , ma'am .
I am .

841
01:25:27,622 --> 01:25:30,751
I really just don't want anyone
to get hurt.

842
01:25:30,917 --> 01:25:32,878
It's not that.

843
01:25:33,837 --> 01:25:35,756
It's just. . . .

844
01:25:41,219 --> 01:25:42,846
There's no money .

845
01:25:43,638 --> 01:25:47,517
We're completely bankrupt.
Don't tell anybody .

846
01:25:47,684 --> 01:25:51,688
lt turned out that the Savings and Loan
had already been robbed . . .

847
01:25:51,855 --> 01:25:56,360
. . . not by armed bandits, but
by speculators in Texas real estate .

848
01:25:56,526 --> 01:25:58,528
All right, let's go !

849
01:26:09,706 --> 01:26:11,750
Norther!

850
01:26:31,353 --> 01:26:34,106
Yeah ! There's gotta be
close to $400 here !

851
01:26:34,272 --> 01:26:36,775
And that's just from the drawers .

852
01:26:36,942 --> 01:26:39,444
Let's see what you got
from the vault.

853
01:26:49,329 --> 01:26:51,957
This is it? The whole vault?

854
01:26:52,124 --> 01:26:54,459
I'm afraid so .

855
01:26:57,212 --> 01:26:59,131
It's got your deposit slip on it.

856
01:26:59,297 --> 01:27:02,175
Well , I just didn't want you
leaving empty-handed .

857
01:27:02,342 --> 01:27:06,138
There's something you should know.
The reason they don't have money . . . .

858
01:27:06,304 --> 01:27:09,558
l told Norther about
the vagaries of Texas oil money . . .

859
01:27:09,725 --> 01:27:12,602
. . . and its effect on real-estate prices. . .

860
01:27:12,769 --> 01:27:15,939
. . . and how lax enforcement
of fiduciary process. . .

861
01:27:16,106 --> 01:27:19,901
. . . had made savings and loans
particularly vulnerable .

862
01:27:20,068 --> 01:27:24,865
Hearing this news,
Norther was left with one conclusion :

863
01:27:25,031 --> 01:27:28,243
I should go to Wall Street.
That's where all the money is.

864
01:27:28,410 --> 01:27:31,997
l knew then that while my days
as a criminal were over. . .

865
01:27:32,164 --> 01:27:33,457
Thanks for the hand !

866
01:27:33,623 --> 01:27:35,625
. . . Norther's were just beginning .

867
01:27:35,792 --> 01:27:38,211
When Norther made
his first million dollars. . .

868
01:27:38,378 --> 01:27:40,672
. . . he sent me a cheque for 1 0 , 000 .

869
01:27:40,839 --> 01:27:44,926
l protested , but he said it was my fee
as his career advisor.

870
01:27:45,093 --> 01:27:48,263
lt was enough to buy my wife
a proper house . . .

871
01:27:48,430 --> 01:27:50,515
. . .with a white picket fence .

872
01:27:50,682 --> 01:27:54,853
And for that, it was all the riches
a man could ever want.

873
01:28:28,720 --> 01:28:30,680
I was drying out.

874
01:28:32,432 --> 01:28:34,351
I see .

875
01:28:36,520 --> 01:28:40,732
I think we ought to get you
a plant mister. . .

876
01:28:40,899 --> 01:28:43,693
. . . so we can just spray you
like a fern .

877
01:29:09,594 --> 01:29:12,097
Come , now.

878
01:29:16,434 --> 01:29:19,813
I don't think I'll ever dry out.

879
01:30:17,996 --> 01:30:21,041
You carry on . I'll see . . . .

880
01:30:25,712 --> 01:30:27,505
Hello .

881
01:30:28,089 --> 01:30:32,010
-Are you Jennifer H ill?
-I am .

882
01:30:32,177 --> 01:30:34,346
And you're Will .

883
01:30:34,512 --> 01:30:37,515
I've seen a picture of you .
That's how I recognise you .

884
01:30:40,185 --> 01:30:42,854
Listen , Kenny ,
why don't we skip the lesson today?

885
01:30:43,021 --> 01:30:45,357
We can go again ne xt week.

886
01:30:45,523 --> 01:30:47,525
Do I have to give it back to my mom?

887
01:30:47,692 --> 01:30:50,278
Well , I won't tell her if you won't.

888
01:31:01,706 --> 01:31:03,208
How did you know my father?

889
01:31:03,583 --> 01:31:05,669
Well , this was on his sales route . . .

890
01:31:05,835 --> 01:31:09,881
. . . so he was through here all the time ,
and everyone in town knew him .

891
01:31:10,048 --> 01:31:12,133
Were you having an affair?

892
01:31:14,552 --> 01:31:16,972
Wow. Wow, you just said it.

893
01:31:17,764 --> 01:31:21,393
I was e xpecting to dance
around this for another half-hour.

894
01:31:23,228 --> 01:31:26,231
I've seen him with women .
He flirts . He always has .

895
01:31:26,398 --> 01:31:30,568
And on some level ,
I just presumed that he was . . .

896
01:31:30,735 --> 01:31:34,239
. . . cheating on my mom .
I just never had proof.

897
01:31:36,908 --> 01:31:38,827
Well , can I ask you a question?

898
01:31:38,994 --> 01:31:41,746
If you found this deed ,
why didn't you just ask Eddie?

899
01:31:42,872 --> 01:31:45,208
Because he's dying .

900
01:31:53,925 --> 01:31:58,596
Look, I don't know how much
you want to know about any of this .

901
01:31:58,763 --> 01:32:00,890
You have one image of your father. . .

902
01:32:01,057 --> 01:32:03,309
. . . and it'd be wrong of me
to change it. . .

903
01:32:03,476 --> 01:32:05,437
. . . especially this late in the game .

904
01:32:05,687 --> 01:32:08,064
My father talked about things
he never did . . .

905
01:32:08,231 --> 01:32:11,609
. . . and I'm sure he did a lot of things
that he never talked about.

906
01:32:11,776 --> 01:32:13,778
I'm just trying to reconcile the two .

907
01:32:17,240 --> 01:32:20,326
The first thing
you have to understand . . .

908
01:32:20,493 --> 01:32:23,455
. . . is that your father
never meant to end up here .

909
01:32:24,039 --> 01:32:25,415
Yet he did .

910
01:32:25,623 --> 01:32:27,292
Twice .

911
01:32:27,834 --> 01:32:30,336
The first time , he was early .

912
01:32:30,503 --> 01:32:32,756
The second time , he was late .

913
01:32:34,841 --> 01:32:38,762
Those days, your father was working
for himself.

914
01:32:38,928 --> 01:32:41,848
lf there's one thing you could
say about Edward Bloom . . .

915
01:32:42,015 --> 01:32:47,020
. . . it's that he was a social person ,
and people took a liking to him .

916
01:32:47,187 --> 01:32:50,440
One night, he was returning
from three weeks on the road . . .

917
01:32:50,607 --> 01:32:54,319
. . .when he hit a thunderstorm
unlike any in his life .

918
01:34:42,594 --> 01:34:45,138
Fate has a way of circling back
on a man . . .

919
01:34:45,305 --> 01:34:47,432
. . . and taking him by surprise .

920
01:34:52,145 --> 01:34:55,815
A man sees things differently
at different times in his life .

921
01:34:55,982 --> 01:34:59,944
This town didn't seem the same
now that he was older.

922
01:35:01,112 --> 01:35:04,657
A new road had brought
the outside world to Spectre . . .

923
01:35:04,824 --> 01:35:07,952
. . . and with it, banks, liens and debt.

924
01:35:08,119 --> 01:35:12,957
Almost everywhere you looked ,
people were bankrupt.

925
01:35:14,250 --> 01:35:17,629
The auction today
is for the town of Spectre .

926
01:35:17,795 --> 01:35:20,506
-The opening bid will be $ 1 0 , 000 .
-So Edward Bloom . . .

927
01:35:20,673 --> 01:35:23,301
- . . .decided to buy the town .
-50 , 000 .

928
01:35:27,472 --> 01:35:30,683
-I couldn't believe my eyes--
-He was never a wealthy man . . .

929
01:35:30,850 --> 01:35:36,022
. . . but he had made other men rich ,
and now he asked for their favours.

930
01:35:37,941 --> 01:35:40,985
-I'm trying to save . . . .
-Most of them hadn't seen Spectre .

931
01:35:41,152 --> 01:35:44,364
They only had Edward's words
to describe it.

932
01:35:44,530 --> 01:35:47,825
That's all he needed .
He sold them on the dream.

933
01:35:48,243 --> 01:35:50,745
So first he bought the farms.

934
01:35:50,912 --> 01:35:54,832
Then he bought the houses.
And then he bought the stores.

935
01:35:55,416 --> 01:36:00,505
Whatever he bought, the people were
not asked to leave or pay rent.

936
01:36:00,672 --> 01:36:03,591
They were just asked to keep
doing as they were doing .

937
01:36:03,967 --> 01:36:08,012
ln that way , he could make sure
the town would never die.

938
01:36:10,431 --> 01:36:15,687
Within six months, his trust had
purchased the entire town .

939
01:36:15,853 --> 01:36:18,523
